---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { RadiusDemo } from '../../components/RadiusDemo';
import { MoodSwitcher } from '../../components/MoodSwitcher';
import { catalog } from '@subtle/catalog';
const foundations = catalog.filter(item => item.kind === 'foundation');
---
<Layout path="/foundations/">
  <div class="wrap page-intro"><p class="kicker">The shared grammar</p><h1>Start with<br />what stays consistent.</h1><p class="lead">Color sets the atmosphere. Type, shape, and proportion keep everything recognizably Subtle.</p></div>
  <div class="wrap foundation-layout"><nav class="page-index" aria-label="Foundation topics">{foundations.map(item => <a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.title}</a>)}</nav><div class="foundation-content">
    <section id="color" class="reference-section"><h2>Color and contrast</h2><p>Eight canonical palettes share the same semantic roles. Try a mood to see them applied here.</p><MoodSwitcher client:load /><div class="surface-ladder"><div class="surface-canvas">Canvas<span>bg-main</span></div><div class="surface-low">Low<span>bg-surface-variant</span></div><div class="surface-normal">Surface<span>bg-surface</span></div><div class="surface-high">High<span>primary-container</span></div><div class="surface-floating">Floating<span>bg-surface / level 2</span></div></div><p class="reference-note">Canonical colors remain intact. Readable supporting text uses a documented mix of muted and main text. Rosewood also supplies explicit accessibility correction tokens. Text-light is reserved for decoration.</p><a class="text-link" href="/moods/">Inspect the mood tokens ↗</a></section>
    <section id="typography" class="reference-section"><h2>Typography</h2><p>Lexend Deca gives headings their rounded character. Inclusive Sans handles reading and controls. JetBrains Mono identifies technical values.</p><div class="type-specimen"><span class="product-meta">Lexend Deca / Editorial</span><div class="type-display">Room to think.</div><span class="product-meta">Inclusive Sans / Body</span><p class="type-body">A softer surface, a generous margin, a clear next step. Small decisions make an interface feel considered.</p><span class="product-meta">JetBrains Mono / Metadata</span><code>--radius-lg: 24px;</code></div><div class="type-comparison"><div><h3>Editorial scale</h3><p>Display 56–80 px<br />H1 44–60 px<br />H2 32–44 px<br />Body 16–18 px</p></div><div><h3>Product scale</h3><p>Title 28–36 px<br />Section 20–24 px<br />Card 16–18 px<br />Body 14–16 px</p></div></div><p class="product-meta">Responsive clamp() values connect the scales across viewport widths.</p></section>
    <section id="shape" class="reference-section"><h2>Shape communicates hierarchy.</h2><p>Step down from an anchor surface to its nested controls. Save full rounding for deliberate capsules.</p><div class="shape-ladder">{[['xs',6],['sm',10],['md',16],['lg',24],['xl',32],['full',9999]].map(([name,value]) => <div><span style={`border-radius:${value}px`}></span><strong>{name}</strong><small>{name === 'full' ? 'Capsule' : `${value} px`}</small></div>)}</div><p class="reference-note">Inputs and buttons use 10 px. Cards use 16 px. Inspectors use 24 px. Hero media uses 32 px.</p><h3>Concentric corners</h3><p><code>R_outer = R_inner + padding</code> (outer radius = inner radius + inset). For a 24 px outer corner and 8 px padding, use a 16 px inner radius. Count the border thickness when measuring the inset. Clamp the inner radius at zero when the inset exceeds the outer radius.</p><RadiusDemo client:visible /><p>This is a useful geometric heuristic for circular CSS corners with a uniform inset. CSS <code>border-radius</code> draws circular or elliptical arcs and can clamp overlapping radii. A superellipse is a useful comparison for continuous curves, not an exact description of Apple’s geometry.</p><p>Jitendra Kumar’s <a class="text-link" href="https://medium.com/design-bootcamp/getting-your-border-radius-right-a-simple-trick-for-smooth-nested-containers-f6e0025e8c53">Getting Your Border Radius Right: A Simple Trick for Smooth Nested Containers</a> explains the padding heuristic and suggests optical adjustments for deeper nesting. Unequal padding, outlines, capsules, and independent close controls still need judgment. A control’s hit target is separate from the nested-surface formula.</p><p>Apple’s official SwiftUI documentation describes <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/roundedcornerstyle/continuous">continuous corners</a> as continuous-curvature corners and <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/concentricrectangle">ConcentricRectangle</a> as a shape that can resolve corner radii relative to a container. These are native SwiftUI APIs; this CSS circular-corner demo does not implement them.</p></section>
    <section id="spacing" class="reference-section"><h2>Space is part of the composition.</h2><p>Use the shared scale to separate the story from the controls. Product spacing groups a task; editorial spacing gives it context.</p><div class="spacing-ladder">{[4,8,12,16,24,32,48,64,96,128].map(value => <div><span style={`width:${value}px`}></span><code>{value}</code></div>)}</div></section>
    <section id="surfaces" class="reference-section"><h2>Tone before shadow.</h2><p>Low maps to the surface variant. High maps to the primary container. Floating keeps the surface color and adds restrained depth.</p><div class="elevation-ladder">{[0,1,2,3].map(level => <div style={`box-shadow:var(--shadow-${level},none)`}><strong>Level {level}</strong><span>{['Open composition','Quiet separation','Floating context','Foreground task'][level]}</span></div>)}</div></section>
    <section id="density" class="reference-section"><h2>Two contexts, one system.</h2><p>Editorial uses larger type, wider spacing, and fewer containers. Product gives working data and controls more containment.</p><a href="/#modes" class="text-link">Compare the live specimens ↗</a></section>
    <section id="motion" class="reference-section"><h2>Softly responsive.</h2><p>Use short transitions to explain state. Remove spatial travel when reduced motion is requested.</p><a href="/motion/" class="text-link">Try the motion vocabulary ↗</a></section>
  </div></div>
</Layout>
